var e=[{id:`meet_review_1`,title:`Weekly Review`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`The boss waves you in before you can knock. 'Miller! This is Mr Brandtner and Ms Vogl of Synerqon Consulting.' He says 'Synerqon' the way other people say 'the Vatican'. 'These good people are accompanying our efficiency transformation. Do tell us: how did your week go?'`,opts:[{t:`Sum the week up honestly`,next:`berater`},{t:`'All in the green.'`,next:`gruen`}]},root_gala:{text:`The boss waves you in before you can knock. 'Miller! This is Mr Brandtner and Ms Vogl of Synerqon Consulting.' He says 'Synerqon' the way other people say 'the Vatican'.
'And let us keep it short,' says Ms Vogl, without looking up from her tablet. 'The Synergy Gala starts at 16:30. We are expected.' The boss nods so hard that his glasses slip. 'So, Miller: how did your week go?'`,opts:[{t:`Sum the week up honestly`,next:`berater`},{t:`'All in the green.'`,next:`gruen`}]},berater:{char:`Frau Vogl (Synerqon)`,text:`'Interesting,' says Ms Vogl, typing. 'So a classic resource alignment deficit with a reactive escalation culture.' Mr Brandtner nods as though he had been listening. 'We recommend a framework.'`,opts:[{t:`'We do not need a framework. We need staff.'`,next:`klartext_folgen`},{t:`Nod earnestly and use the word 'fascinating'`,next:`folien`}]},gruen:{char:`Herr Brandtner (Synerqon)`,text:`'In the green,' Mr Brandtner repeats slowly, as though testing the phrase for contaminants. 'Our figures show 40 per cent friction loss in your department.' Where he gets figures about your department nobody knows. The boss takes notes.`,opts:[{t:`Question the 40 per cent`,next:`quelle_frage`},{t:`Take the 40 per cent contritely on board`,next:`commitment`}]},klartext_folgen:{char:`Frau Vogl (Synerqon)`,text:`Ms Vogl puts the tablet down — a gesture that in the consulting trade evidently signifies gravity. 'Staff,' she says pleasantly. 'You see, that is precisely where the misunderstanding begins. Staff is a symptom.' She writes SYMPTOM on the flipchart and underlines it. The boss copies it down.`,opts:[{t:`Stick with it and count out the open tickets`,next:`res_klartext`},{t:`'A symptom, of course. That is what I meant.'`,next:`res_einlenken`}]},folien:{char:`Herr Brandtner (Synerqon)`,text:`'Fascinating' was the cue. Mr Brandtner opens the presentation. Slide 3 shows three circles, slide 7 the same circles with arrows. At slide 19 you hear the fan in your own laptop start up in the next room. At slide 24 the boss asks whether there are any questions.`,opts:[{t:`No questions. Sit it out.`,next:`res_nicken`},{t:`Ask a question to speed it up`,next:`res_frage`}]},quelle_frage:{char:`Herr Brandtner (Synerqon)`,text:`'Gladly,' says Mr Brandtner, turning the laptop round. An Excel file, three tabs, a pivot table. The figures come from the intranet dashboard. You can tell by a column that has returned nothing but zeros since the rebuild in 2019. The boss leans forward and nods, impressed.`,opts:[{t:`Let him have it`,next:`res_zweifel`},{t:`Say where the figures come from`,next:`res_aufdecken`}]},commitment:{char:`Frau Vogl (Synerqon)`,text:`'Good that we agree.' Ms Vogl turns to a slide headed COMMITMENT. Below it three empty lines and a date two weeks away. 'What will you commit to specifically?' The boss looks at you. Everyone looks at you.`,opts:[{t:`Say something vague that sounds good`,next:`res_zerknirscht`},{t:`Commit to nothing you cannot deliver`,next:`res_ausweichen`}]}},results:{res_klartext:{txt:`Silence. Ms Vogl shuts the tablet. 'That is exactly the silo thinking we mean.' The boss apologises to her — to her, not to you. Outside at the copier stands Kevin, holding a thumb up at you through the glass wall.`,rep:{Kevin:5},m:50,l:0,a:10,b:5},res_nicken:{txt:`At slide 31 the boss stops taking notes, at slide 38 he stops nodding. Your department gets a framework regardless. It is called SYNERGATE, and nobody will ever mention it again — except at the next review.`,m:55,l:10,a:5,b:-5},res_zweifel:{txt:`You let him have it. He gets his nod, the boss gets his figure, and you keep the one thing that is scarce in this room: time. Some victories are won quietly.`,m:45,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_zerknirscht:{txt:`You nod contritely. The boss nods with relief. The consultants nod with satisfaction. For a moment the whole room nods in time, and somewhere a slide headed 'Commitment' comes of it.`,m:45,l:5,a:5,b:-5},res_einlenken:{txt:`'A symptom, of course.' Ms Vogl smiles as though she had taught you something. Which she has. The flipchart now reads SYMPTOM, and underneath it your name in brackets. The boss considers it to have been a productive conversation.`,m:50,l:5,a:10,b:-5},res_frage:{txt:`Your question was meant as a shortcut. Mr Brandtner is visibly delighted and jumps back to slide 7 to answer it in context. It takes twelve minutes. The boss looks at you as though you had done it on purpose.`,m:60,l:0,a:15,b:0},res_aufdecken:{txt:`You name the dashboard, the year it was built and the column full of zeros. Mr Brandtner turns the page without looking up. The boss asks again, three times, and on the third he understands. On Monday there is an email in your inbox: request to review the data basis, consultants in CC.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:55,l:-5,a:5,b:10},res_ausweichen:{txt:`'I would rather not commit to something I cannot deliver.' The line on the slide stays empty. Ms Vogl notes that too, in a list of her own. The boss says nothing the whole way to the lift, and as he steps out he says: 'That was correct.' By his standards that is a decoration.`,m:45,l:0,a:-5,b:5}}},{id:`meet_synergie_1`,title:`Strategy Sync Q3`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`'Miller, on time!' The boss sounds surprised. 'Mr Petersen of McKandy and Partners.' Mr Petersen looks like three other consultants you have met this year. Perhaps he is them. 'Chantal is doing the minutes today.' Chantal has already stopped typing.`,opts:[{t:`Offer the weekly report`,next:`bericht`},{t:`Wait and see what Mr Petersen has in mind`,next:`petersen`}]},root_gala:{text:`'Miller, on time!' The boss sounds surprised. 'Mr Petersen of McKandy and Partners. Chantal is doing the minutes.'
'Before we begin,' says Mr Petersen, straightening his cuffs, 'we shall keep this compact. The Synergy Gala starts on time, and I am giving the opening address.' The boss applauds briefly and alone.`,opts:[{t:`Offer the weekly report`,next:`bericht`},{t:`Wait and see what Mr Petersen has in mind`,next:`petersen`}]},bericht:{char:`Chantal`,text:`You begin. After one sentence Chantal raises her hand. 'Slower. I'm writing: Miller ... reports ... on ... things.' Mr Petersen notes something down as well. It looks longer than your sentence.`,opts:[{t:`Carry on slowly, at minute-taking speed`,next:`diktat`},{t:`'I'll send the minutes on by email later.'`,next:`asynchron`}]},petersen:{char:`Herr Petersen (McKandy & Partner)`,text:`'I have analysed your week,' says Mr Petersen, turning his laptop round. On it: a pie chart with three segments. 'Potential. Synergy. And this here' — he taps the largest segment — 'Delta.' The boss photographs the slide with his phone.`,opts:[{t:`Ask what 'Delta' actually means`,next:`workshop`},{t:`Praise the chart`,next:`kugelschreiber`}]},diktat:{char:`Chantal`,text:`You speak at dictation pace. Chantal keeps up, word for word. At 'failure of the air conditioning' she raises her hand again: 'Failure — how do you write that in the minutes? Technically, I mean?' Mr Petersen looks up. For the first time something interests him.`,opts:[{t:`Dictate it technically correctly`,next:`res_protokoll`},{t:`'Just write: incident.'`,next:`res_vorfall`},{t:`Let Chantal write the minutes on her own`,next:`res_chantal_allein`}]},asynchron:{char:`Herr Petersen (McKandy)`,text:`'Asynchronous communication,' Mr Petersen repeats, making a note. 'Bold.' Then, pleasantly: 'Might I be copied in on that email? For taking stock.' The boss nods at once. The question was actually put to you.`,opts:[{t:`Agree, and word the email carefully accordingly`,next:`res_mail`},{t:`'These are internal minutes.'`,next:`res_intern`}]},workshop:{char:`Herr Petersen (McKandy)`,text:`'Delta is the gap between actual and target.' You ask what actual and target are. 'We define that in the workshop.' On the next slide the workshop is already there with a date, a duration and a daily rate. The boss reads the figure and says 'mm'.`,opts:[{t:`Work out what the daily rate means in posts`,next:`res_delta`},{t:`Leave it at that`,next:`res_mhm`}]},kugelschreiber:{char:`Herr Petersen (McKandy)`,text:`Mr Petersen beams and presents you with a McKandy pen, heavy, with the logo on it. The boss sees the pen. The boss would like one too. There is another on the table, within reach, and you both know it.`,opts:[{t:`Slide the second one over to the boss`,next:`res_lob`},{t:`Do nothing and pocket your own`,next:`res_stift`}]}},results:{res_protokoll:{txt:`You speak at dictation pace. Chantal keeps up, Mr Petersen sleeps with his eyes open, and at the end the boss finds your weekly report 'very structured'. The minutes contain two spelling mistakes and the word 'things' once.`,rep:{Chantal:5},m:50,l:5,a:5,b:-5},res_mail:{txt:`'Email is fine,' says Chantal and shuts her laptop. Mr Petersen lifts an eyebrow: 'Asynchronous communication. Bold.' It does not sound like a compliment, but the boss notes it down as an innovation impulse.`,m:45,l:-5,a:0,b:-5},res_delta:{txt:`'Delta,' says Mr Petersen, 'is the gap between actual and target.' You ask what actual and target are. 'We define that in the workshop.' The workshop costs the budget of four posts. Yours is not among them for now. For now.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:0},res_lob:{txt:`'Very clear chart.' Mr Petersen beams and presents you with a McKandy pen. The boss sees the pen. The boss would like a pen too. You can feel it: this will come up again.`,m:45,l:5,a:0,b:5},res_vorfall:{txt:`'Incident' now appears in the minutes, seven times. It reads as though something dreadful had happened this week, and in a way that is true. Mr Petersen underlines the word and asks whether there are frequency statistics for it.`,m:45,l:5,a:5,b:5},res_intern:{txt:`'Internal minutes' brings the room to a brief halt. Mr Petersen nods understandingly, Chantal's eyes go from one of you to the other. In the corridor afterwards the boss says that was 'perhaps a little narrow'. You send the email internally only, all the same.`,m:45,l:0,a:0,b:10},res_mhm:{txt:`You say nothing, the boss says 'mm', the workshop is in the calendar. On the way back you work it out after all, in your head, and arrive at four posts. It helps nobody that you now know.`,m:45,l:5,a:10,b:-5},res_stift:{txt:`You pocket yours. The second one stays where it is until Mr Petersen packs it away again while clearing up. The boss sees it. He says nothing, but he has seen it, and that pen will come up again.`,m:45,l:0,a:0,b:10},res_chantal_allein:{txt:`You leave the minutes to Chantal. She writes them alone and reads them out at the end. They are astonishingly short, astonishingly precise and contain not one word of Mr Petersen's. He does not notice.`,m:40,l:10,a:0,b:0}}},{id:`meet_kennzahlen_1`,title:`Metrics Deep-Dive`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`In the meeting room, beside the boss, sits a single consultant — and Dr Wichtig in person. The boss is sitting so upright it must hurt. 'Miller. Mr Berger of Synerqon. And our CEO is taking the time today in person, no less.' Dr Wichtig looks up from his phone: 'Who are you?'
The door opens: Kevin, with a plate from the buffet that does not exist here.`,opts:[{t:`'The week's figures are ready.'`,next:`zahlen`},{t:`Sit down next to Kevin, inconspicuously`,next:`kevin`}]},root_gala:{text:`In the meeting room, beside the boss, sits a single consultant — and Dr Wichtig in person. The boss is sitting so upright it must hurt. 'Miller. Mr Berger of Synerqon.'
'One thing first,' says Dr Wichtig, standing up although nobody has asked him to. 'Tonight is the Synergy Gala. My idea. So we shall be quick here.' The boss starts to applaud, notices, stops.
The door opens: Kevin, with a plate from the buffet that does not exist here.`,opts:[{t:`'The week's figures are ready.'`,next:`zahlen`},{t:`Sit down next to Kevin, inconspicuously`,next:`kevin`}]},zahlen:{char:`Herr Berger (Synerqon)`,text:`'We have normalised your week,' says Mr Berger. Your week appears on the projector as a waterfall chart. Between Wednesday and Thursday it has a colour that officially does not exist in PowerPoint. 'Send me that one,' says Dr Wichtig.`,opts:[{t:`Ask about the data source`,next:`datenlake`},{t:`Simply let the chart do its work`,next:`wachstumsstory`}]},kevin:{char:`Kevin`,text:`Kevin leans over and whispers: 'Is that the real Wichtig?' You nod. Kevin looks at the CEO for a long moment. 'I thought he'd be taller.' Dr Wichtig glances across. Kevin studies his plate with great concentration.`,opts:[{t:`'Concentrate, Kevin.'`,next:`kevin_malt`},{t:`Agree in a whisper`,next:`augenbraue`}]},datenlake:{char:`Herr Berger (Synerqon)`,text:`'From your data lake.' You have no data lake. You have a folder called NEU_FINAL_2. Dr Wichtig notes down 'data lake' with two exclamation marks and asks whether it could be expanded.`,opts:[{t:`Leave it there`,next:`res_quelle`},{t:`Explain what is really in that folder`,next:`res_ordner`}]},wachstumsstory:{char:`Dr. Wichtig`,text:`The chart does its work. Dr Wichtig stands up, positions himself beside the screen and points at the unlabelled bar. 'And this here,' he says, 'is our potential.' It is the Thursday the air conditioning failed.`,opts:[{t:`Agree and call the bar potential`,next:`res_wirken`},{t:`Say what the bar actually is`,next:`res_donnerstag`},{t:`Say nothing and look at the clock`,next:`res_uhr_kenn`}]},kevin_malt:{char:`Kevin`,text:`For two minutes Kevin is the most focused member of the gathering. Then he starts copying out the waterfall chart and giving the colours names. Mr Berger sees it and leans forward with interest: 'Is your colleague doing a visualisation?'`,opts:[{t:`'Yes. A visualisation.'`,next:`res_konzentrier`},{t:`Tell the truth`,next:`res_malen`}]},augenbraue:{char:`Dr. Wichtig`,text:`Kevin snorts into his plate. Everyone turns to look at you both. Dr Wichtig raises an eyebrow, very slowly, like a man with time on his hands. 'You said something, Miller. Do share it with all of us.'`,opts:[{t:`Play it down`,next:`res_zustimmen`},{t:`Repeat it out loud`,next:`res_laut`}]}},results:{res_quelle:{txt:`'From your data lake,' says Mr Berger. You have no data lake. You have a folder called 'NEU_FINAL_2'. You leave well alone. Dr Wichtig notes down 'data lake' with two exclamation marks.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:0},res_wirken:{txt:`The chart does its work. Dr Wichtig is impressed, the boss is relieved, and your week is now officially a 'growth story with points of friction'. It sounds better than it felt.`,rep:{"Dr. Wichtig":5},m:45,l:5,a:-5,b:-5},res_konzentrier:{txt:`Kevin nods and for two minutes is the most focused member of the gathering. Then he copies out the waterfall chart and gives the colours names. The unknown one he calls 'Thursday'.`,rep:{Kevin:5},m:45,l:5,a:-5,b:0},res_zustimmen:{txt:`'True,' you whisper, 'he looks taller on the intranet.' Kevin snorts into his plate. Everyone turns to look at you both. Dr Wichtig raises an eyebrow, very slowly, like a man with time on his hands.`,rep:{Kevin:5,"Dr. Wichtig":-5},m:45,l:5,a:-5,b:10},res_ordner:{txt:`You explain NEU_FINAL_2, including the subfolders NEU_FINAL_2_alt and NEU_FINAL_2_wirklich. Mr Berger takes it down. Dr Wichtig stops making notes. On the way back the boss asks whether that could not be 'tidied up', and means you.`,m:55,l:-5,a:5,b:5},res_donnerstag:{txt:`'That is the Thursday with the air conditioning.' Dr Wichtig looks at the bar, then at you. 'Potential as well,' he says, and sits down. From now on the bar is called potential, but everyone in the room now knows what it really was.`,m:50,l:0,a:-5,b:5},res_malen:{txt:`'He is copying it out.' A short silence. Then Mr Berger laughs, the only one, loudly and a shade too long. Kevin pushes the sheet away and is not seen again for the rest of the session. On Monday it is lying folded on your desk.`,rep:{Kevin:-5},m:45,l:0,a:5,b:0},res_laut:{txt:`'He looks taller on the intranet.' You say it into a room that is silent. The boss draws breath. Dr Wichtig looks at you for a long moment and then says: 'The photograph is from 2011.' It sounds almost conciliatory. Kevin beams as though you had conquered a kingdom.`,rep:{Kevin:10,"Dr. Wichtig":-5},m:45,l:0,a:-10,b:15},res_uhr_kenn:{txt:`You look at the clock, and Dr Wichtig sees you look at the clock. The bar stays potential, your gaze stays on the minutes. It is not written there, but everyone in the room saw it.`,m:40,l:5,a:5,b:5}}},{id:`meet_beamer_1`,title:`Tech Check`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`The boss is standing in front of the screen, pressing buttons on the remote. Beside him Ms Kienzle of Norden & Kessler waits with a USB stick in her hand. The projector is showing a blue image and the time from the day before yesterday. 'There you are, Miller. You are the expert, after all.'`,opts:[{t:`Go over to the projector`,next:`beamer`},{t:`Stay seated`,next:`warten`}]},root_gala:{text:`The boss is standing in front of the screen, pressing buttons on the remote. Beside him Ms Kienzle of Norden & Kessler waits with a USB stick in her hand. The projector is showing a blue image and the time from the day before yesterday.
'We have forty minutes,' says Ms Kienzle. 'After that the Synergy Gala begins, and I am travelling there with the board.' The boss presses faster. 'There you are, Miller. You are the expert, after all.'`,opts:[{t:`Go over to the projector`,next:`beamer`},{t:`Stay seated`,next:`warten`}]},beamer:{char:`Egon`,text:`The cable is in. The projector is on HDMI 2, the laptop is sending on HDMI 1. You have the remote in your hand when Egon comes in with a ladder. 'Boss said the projector's broken.' He looks up. 'That thing's been up there eight years.'`,opts:[{t:`'Two clicks and it runs.'`,next:`zwei_klicks`},{t:`Let Egon set the ladder up`,next:`leiter`}]},warten:{char:`Frau Kienzle (Norden & Kessler)`,text:`Ms Kienzle tries it herself. First the stick, then a different port, then the stick again, more slowly. The boss says 'yes, exactly' to suggestions nobody has made. After six minutes she turns round.`,opts:[{t:`Get up now`,next:`sechs_minuten`},{t:`'You are doing well.'`,next:`kienzle_probiert`}]},zwei_klicks:{char:`Frau Kienzle (Norden & Kessler)`,text:`Two clicks and the picture is there. Ms Kienzle thanks the boss. The boss accepts the thanks. Then she asks whether the room could not be made permanently 'presentation-ready' — she knows somebody.`,opts:[{t:`Say nothing`,next:`res_eingang`},{t:`'The room is presentation-ready. The input was wrong.'`,next:`res_richtigstellen`}]},leiter:{char:`Egon`,text:`Egon sets up the ladder, climbs it and switches the input over on the unit itself. From above, with a biro. The picture is there. 'Was the wiring,' he says to the boss, who nods, impressed. From up on the ladder Egon looks at you and waits.`,opts:[{t:`Nod. It was the wiring.`,next:`res_leiter`},{t:`Reveal what he actually did`,next:`res_aufloesen`},{t:`Act as though you had not been looking`,next:`res_weggesehen`}]},sechs_minuten:{char:`Frau Kienzle (Norden & Kessler)`,text:`You go over and press twice. The picture is there. 'I would have managed that too,' says Ms Kienzle. The boss notes something down, and from here you can read what: six minutes, IT response time.`,opts:[{t:`Let it stand`,next:`res_spaet`},{t:`Point out that nobody called`,next:`res_gerufen`}]},kienzle_probiert:{char:`Frau Kienzle (Norden & Kessler)`,text:`She looks at you, then at the projector, then at you again. Two clicks later the picture is there, and she says nothing further about it. Slide 9 is headed DIFFUSION OF RESPONSIBILITY. The boss finds it particularly apt.`,opts:[{t:`Sit through the slide without comment`,next:`res_lob`},{t:`Ask whether slide 9 came out of experience`,next:`res_folie9`}]}},results:{res_eingang:{txt:`Two clicks and the picture is there. Ms Kienzle thanks the boss. You say nothing, and the boss accepts the thanks. The quotation for the 'presentation-ready room' is in your inbox the next day, forwarded, with one word: 'Review.'`,m:45,l:0,a:10,b:-5},res_leiter:{txt:`Egon sets up the ladder, climbs it and switches the input over on the unit itself. From above, with a biro. The picture is there. 'Was the wiring,' he says to the boss, who nods, impressed.`,rep:{Egon:5},m:50,l:5,a:0,b:-5},res_spaet:{txt:`You go over and press twice. The picture is there. 'I would have managed that too,' says Ms Kienzle. The boss notes the six minutes down as IT response time.`,m:50,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_lob:{txt:`She looks at you, then at the projector, then at you again. Two clicks later the picture is there, and she says nothing further about it. Slide 9 is about the diffusion of responsibility.`,m:50,l:5,a:0,b:5},res_richtigstellen:{txt:`You say it plainly, in one sentence. Ms Kienzle notes nothing. The boss notes something. The room stays presentation-ready, the quotation comes anyway, three weeks later, by email, with you in CC.`,m:50,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_aufloesen:{txt:`'He switched the input over.' Egon climbs down without looking at you, folds the ladder and goes. The boss does not understand it and asks about it later. Egon still greets you on Monday, but he says nothing about it, and that is the message.`,rep:{Egon:-5},m:45,l:0,a:5,b:0},res_gerufen:{txt:`'Nobody called me.' The boss looks at his note, then at you, then crosses out the six minutes. Underneath he writes something new that you cannot read from here. Not knowing is usually the better position in this building.`,m:45,l:0,a:-5,b:10},res_folie9:{txt:`'From experience?' Ms Kienzle smiles honestly for the first time. 'From a great deal of experience.' After that the presentation runs noticeably faster, and two slides she skips entirely. The boss does not notice.`,m:40,l:0,a:-10,b:0},res_weggesehen:{txt:`You look away as Egon climbs down from the ladder. It was the wiring, it stays the wiring. On Monday there is a coffee on your desk, with no note. Egon does not drink coffee.`,rep:{Egon:5},m:45,l:0,a:-5,b:0}}},{id:`meet_tool_1`,title:`Product Presentation`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`'Miller, do sit down, this will please you.' Beside the boss sits Mr Sobotka of Norden & Kessler, and next to him Ms Elster with a folder on her lap. On the screen is a logo that looks like your ticket system, only rounder.`,opts:[{t:`Take a closer look at the logo`,next:`logo`},{t:`Look at Ms Elster, who is holding on to the folder`,next:`elster`}]},root_gala:{text:`'Miller, do sit down, this will please you.' Beside the boss sits Mr Sobotka of Norden & Kessler, and next to him Ms Elster with a folder on her lap.
'I shall keep it brief,' says Mr Sobotka, adjusting his tie. 'The Synergy Gala is at 16:30, and I am at the board's table.' On the screen is a logo that looks like your ticket system, only rounder.`,opts:[{t:`Take a closer look at the logo`,next:`logo`},{t:`Look at Ms Elster, who is holding on to the folder`,next:`elster`}]},logo:{char:`Herr Sobotka (Norden & Kessler)`,text:`'Ticketing 4.0,' says Mr Sobotka. 'Cloud-based, AI-assisted, fully integrated.' He clicks on. The interface is yours. The same columns, the same truncated subject line, the same sort order that has been impossible to change for years.`,opts:[{t:`'That is our system with a new logo.'`,next:`individualisiert`},{t:`Ask about the price`,next:`preisfolie`}]},elster:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`Ms Elster holds the folder against her chest. 'I am to countersign this afterwards,' she whispers. 'But there is a figure in it, and I do not know whether it is per year or per month.' Mr Sobotka smiles in your direction.`,opts:[{t:`'Show me the figure.'`,next:`zahl_lesen`},{t:`'Do not sign anything today.'`,next:`elster_legt_weg`}]},individualisiert:{char:`Herr Sobotka (Norden & Kessler)`,text:`'Precisely,' says Mr Sobotka, without missing a beat. 'We have customised it for you.' The boss finds that reasonable. The slide after that puts a figure on the customisation per year. The amount is higher than your post.`,opts:[{t:`Leave it be`,next:`res_logo`},{t:`Compare the amount with your post`,next:`res_stelle`}]},preisfolie:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`The figure is on the slide for three seconds. It has one digit more before the decimal point than you expected. Ms Elster makes a noise. Mr Sobotka clicks on and says: 'But we shall talk about that later.'`,opts:[{t:`Later is good`,next:`res_preis`},{t:`'We shall discuss it now.'`,next:`res_jetzt`},{t:`Let Ms Elster get on with it`,next:`res_elster_macht`}]},zahl_lesen:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`She turns the folder round. Per month. Per user. Below that, smaller, a term of thirty-six months and an automatic renewal. Ms Elster taps the renewal without saying anything.`,opts:[{t:`Read the renewal clause out loud`,next:`res_zahl`},{t:`Nod to her and close the folder`,next:`res_zuklappen`}]},elster_legt_weg:{char:`Herr Sobotka (Norden & Kessler)`,text:`She puts the folder down. When Mr Sobotka asks about the signature, she says: 'Mr Miller is still checking it.' You did not say that. Mr Sobotka turns to you, pleasantly, with a form in his hand: 'When may I expect to hear from you?'`,opts:[{t:`Name a date`,next:`res_nichts`},{t:`Name no date`,next:`res_kein_termin`}]}},results:{res_logo:{txt:`'Precisely,' says Mr Sobotka, without missing a beat. 'We have customised it for you.' The boss finds that reasonable. On the way out he asks you why IT cannot manage something like that itself.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:0},res_preis:{txt:`The figure is on the slide for three seconds. It has one digit more before the decimal point than you expected. Ms Elster makes a noise.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:45,l:0,a:5,b:-5},res_zahl:{txt:`Per month. Per user. Ms Elster writes both down and underlines them twice. The minutes state later that Accounts has registered a need for clarification.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:50,l:0,a:0,b:5},res_nichts:{txt:`She puts the folder down. When Mr Sobotka asks about the signature, she says: 'Mr Miller is still checking it.' You did not say that. You are checking it now.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:50,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_stelle:{txt:`You do not say that it is your post. You say it is 'a post in the building'. The boss works it out regardless — you can see it in his face — and after that says nothing for the whole meeting. Nothing gets signed.`,m:55,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_jetzt:{txt:`'We shall discuss it now.' Mr Sobotka clicks back, slowly, and the price is there again. Forty seconds this time. Ms Elster takes it down. Afterwards the boss says it must be 'evaluated calmly', by which he means: not today.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:55,l:0,a:10,b:0},res_zuklappen:{txt:`You nod to her, she closes the folder. Not a word is said, and the matter is settled all the same. Mr Sobotka packs up and says on his way out that he will be in touch in the new quarter. Ms Elster says: 'He will.'`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:45,l:0,a:-5,b:0},res_kein_termin:{txt:`'I am not giving you a date.' Mr Sobotka smiles as though he had expected it, and puts the form on the table anyway. It is still lying there when everyone has gone. On Monday it is in your pigeonhole, with a sticky note: 'as a reminder'.`,m:45,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_elster_macht:{txt:`You let Ms Elster get on with it. She asks four questions, all about figures, and on the fourth Mr Sobotka turns back a page without being asked. Then he packs up. You said nothing in this meeting and got everything.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:45,l:10,a:-5,b:0}}},{id:`meet_neuling_1`,title:`Taking Stock`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`In the meeting room sits a very young consultant. In front of him a laptop, beside it a printed running order with highlighter on it. The boss is not there yet. Out in the corridor somebody is talking loudly on the phone about capacity.`,opts:[{t:`'Good afternoon. Miller, IT.'`,next:`vorstellen`},{t:`Wait until he starts`,next:`warten`}]},root_gala:{text:`In the meeting room sits a very young consultant. In front of him a laptop, beside it a printed running order with highlighter on it. Out in the corridor somebody is talking loudly on the phone about capacity.
'I am to say,' the consultant reads off the sheet, 'that we are bound by time. Tonight is the Synergy Gala.' He looks up. 'Do you actually go to that?'`,opts:[{t:`'Good afternoon. Miller, IT.'`,next:`vorstellen`},{t:`Wait until he starts`,next:`warten`}]},vorstellen:{char:`Herr Lohmann (Synerqon)`,text:`'Lohmann. First day.' He shakes your hand for too long. Then he turns the sheet away so that you cannot see it, and reads out the first question: 'Where do you see your department in three years?'`,opts:[{t:`'In the same room. With the same printer.'`,next:`notiert_woertlich`},{t:`Take the question seriously`,next:`verliert_faden`}]},warten:{char:`Markus`,text:`Markus comes in without knocking and sits down at the head of the table. 'I have ten minutes.' He looks the young consultant over. 'Are you the digitalisation one?' The consultant looks for the question on his sheet.`,opts:[{t:`Let Markus talk for his ten minutes`,next:`abschlussquoten`},{t:`'He is here to take stock.'`,next:`beauftragt_von`}]},notiert_woertlich:{char:`Herr Lohmann`,text:`He writes it down. Word for word, printer included. Then he looks up: 'And how would you rate the collaboration on a scale of one to ten?' The scale is preprinted on his sheet. Beside the ten there is already a tick.`,opts:[{t:`Give an honest number`,next:`res_drucker`},{t:`Point at the tick`,next:`res_haekchen`}]},verliert_faden:{char:`Herr Lohmann`,text:`You answer for three minutes. He types along, loses the thread and asks whether you could say it again from the beginning. Out in the corridor his colleague carries on talking about capacity, loudly enough for both of you to hear.`,opts:[{t:`Say it again, more slowly`,next:`res_ernst`},{t:`Offer to write it down and send it over`,next:`res_schicken`},{t:`Let him read the running order to the end`,next:`res_ablauf`}]},abschlussquoten:{char:`Markus`,text:`Markus talks for eleven minutes about closing rates. The consultant notes everything down. As Markus leaves, he asks you what a closing is — and whether IT is 'responsible' for it. His pen waits.`,opts:[{t:`'No.'`,next:`res_markus`},{t:`Explain it to him properly`,next:`res_erklaerung`}]},beauftragt_von:{char:`Markus`,text:`'Taking stock,' Markus repeats. 'Commissioned by whom?' The consultant leafs through his papers. He leafs for a while. Then he says a company name that nobody in the room knows, and Markus stands up and goes off to make a call.`,opts:[{t:`Write the name down`,next:`res_erklaeren`},{t:`Offer the young man a coffee`,next:`res_kaffee`}]}},results:{res_drucker:{txt:`He writes it down. Word for word, printer included. Two weeks later the sentence appears in a presentation under the heading 'Voices from the Organisation', and the boss asks you who said it.`,m:45,l:0,a:10,b:5},res_ernst:{txt:`You answer for three minutes. He types along, loses the thread and asks whether you could say it again from the beginning. Out in the corridor his colleague carries on talking about capacity.`,m:55,l:5,a:5,b:-5},res_markus:{txt:`Markus talks for eleven minutes about closing rates. The consultant notes everything down. As Markus leaves, he asks you what a closing is.`,rep:{Markus:5},m:50,l:0,a:0,b:0},res_erklaeren:{txt:`'Taking stock,' Markus repeats. 'Commissioned by whom?' The consultant leafs through his papers. Markus stands up and goes off to make a call. Outside he runs into the consultant's colleague, and they talk for a long time.`,rep:{Markus:-5},m:45,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_haekchen:{txt:`You point at the tick. He goes red to the ears, mumbles something about a template and turns the sheet over. On the back is the same scale, unfilled. After that he asks you two questions that are not on the running order, and those are the better ones.`,m:50,l:0,a:-10,b:0},res_schicken:{txt:`You offer to write it down. He is visibly relieved and takes your email address down wrongly. You send the summary anyway, to him and copied to yourself. It shows up word for word in a report later, under his name.`,m:45,l:-5,a:0,b:-5},res_erklaerung:{txt:`You explain what a closing is, who makes one and what IT has to do with it, namely nothing. He takes all of it down. In the later presentation IT appears under 'sales-adjacent functions', and nobody knows how that could have happened.`,m:55,l:-5,a:10,b:0},res_kaffee:{txt:`You fetch him a coffee. He drinks it in one go and for the first time talks without the running order: first day, second week in the job, the colleague outside is his line manager. Then he shuts the laptop and wants to know whether that will end up in the minutes.`,m:50,l:0,a:-10,b:0},res_ablauf:{txt:`You let him read the printed running order to the end. There are nine questions, and from the sixth they repeat. He notices it himself, speeds up and stops at eight. On his way out he thanks you twice.`,m:55,l:5,a:5,b:-5}}},{id:`meet_umfrage_1`,title:`Staff Survey Results`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`Sabine from HR has a projector, a pie chart and a request. 'The survey has been evaluated!' The slide shows satisfaction by department. IT is the smallest slice of the pie, and it is grey, while all the others are in colour.`,opts:[{t:`Ask how many people from IT took part`,next:`beteiligung`},{t:`Let the grey slice sink in for a moment`,next:`grau`}]},root_gala:{text:`Sabine from HR has a projector, a pie chart and a request. 'The survey has been evaluated!' The boss looks at the clock — the gala is waiting — and says all the same: 'Only briefly.' On the slide IT is the smallest slice, and it is grey.`,opts:[{t:`Ask how many people from IT took part`,next:`beteiligung`},{t:`Let the grey slice sink in for a moment`,next:`grau`}]},beteiligung:{char:`Sabine`,text:`'Two,' says Sabine, without looking it up. 'Of two respondents both are dissatisfied, that is one hundred per cent.' She says it as though the figure were the problem and not the basis for it. The boss notes down 'one hundred per cent'.`,opts:[{t:`Explain what two answers mean statistically`,next:`statistik`},{t:`Ask what the two answers actually say`,next:`antworten`}]},grau:{char:`Sabine`,text:`'Grey is not a judgement,' says Sabine quickly. 'Grey is neutral.' Every other department has a colour. Accounts is turquoise. Sabine clicks on. The next slide is headed MEASURES and already carries three points.`,opts:[{t:`Read the three measures`,next:`massnahmen`},{t:`Ask why IT is grey and the rest are not`,next:`farbe`}]},statistik:{char:`Sabine`,text:`You explain sample size in two calm sentences. Sabine listens and slows down while you do. 'So you are saying the figures are wrong?' The boss looks up from his notes. He has already underlined 'one hundred per cent'.`,opts:[{t:`'The figures are right. They just do not mean anything.'`,next:`res_stichprobe`},{t:`Row back and let the figures stand`,next:`res_zurueck`},{t:`Stay silent and let the boss answer`,next:`res_schweigen_umf`}]},antworten:{char:`Sabine`,text:`Sabine turns to the appendix. Answer one: 'Not enough staff.' Answer two: 'Not enough staff.' The same both times, word for word, from two different people. Sabine says: 'We classified that as an individual opinion.'`,opts:[{t:`Press on how two identical answers are one opinion`,next:`res_einzelmeinung`},{t:`Have it minuted and move on`,next:`res_protokolliert`}]},massnahmen:{char:`Sabine`,text:`Point one: fruit basket. Point two: strengthen the feedback culture. Point three: digital satisfaction app. At point three Sabine looks at you, and the boss looks at you too, and in that second you know who will be looking after the app.`,opts:[{t:`Take the app on before somebody else defines it`,next:`res_app`},{t:`Point to the fruit basket they have had before`,next:`res_obstkorb`}]},farbe:{char:`Sabine`,text:`'Because below five answers no colour is assigned.' Sabine says it pleasantly and without noticing what she has just said. The boss nods, satisfied. The rule is in no footnote, but it explains the whole slide.`,opts:[{t:`Let the matter rest`,next:`res_regel`},{t:`Ask which other departments are below five`,next:`res_nachfragen`},{t:`Photograph the slide`,next:`res_foto`}]}},results:{res_stichprobe:{txt:`'The figures are right. They just do not mean anything.' Sabine is silent a moment too long, then shuts the laptop. The slide with the one hundred per cent never appears again. The one with the fruit basket does.`,m:45,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_zurueck:{txt:`You row back, and the one hundred per cent stays where it is. Two weeks later it is pinned up on the noticeboard, printed out, with an arrow pointing down and the heading 'This is where we start'.`,m:45,l:5,a:10,b:-5},res_einzelmeinung:{txt:`'Two identical answers are two answers.' Sabine actually notes it down. A new paragraph appears in the results report later: 'Staffing situation (mentioned more than once)'. It is the first sentence on the subject ever to make it into a document.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:55,l:-5,a:5,b:5},res_protokolliert:{txt:`You have it minuted and move on. The sentence is in the minutes, the minutes are in the folder, the folder is on the shelf, and at least there it stands.`,m:45,l:10,a:0,b:-5},res_app:{txt:`You agree to look after the app before anybody else puts it out to tender. Sabine is delighted. What you have let yourself in for you will only grasp next quarter, but it will at least work.`,m:55,l:-10,a:10,b:-10},res_obstkorb:{txt:`'We have had the fruit basket before.' A short silence. Sabine says that this time it will be 'accompanied'. The boss thinks accompaniment is important. On the way back you think of the withdrawal list and say nothing.`,m:45,l:0,a:10,b:0},res_regel:{txt:`You let the rule stand. IT stays grey, and grey is neutral. On your way out it strikes you that on this slide neutral sits directly next to dissatisfied, in colour terms.`,m:40,l:5,a:5,b:0},res_nachfragen:{txt:`'Which other departments are below five?' Sabine leafs through. There are four. Three of them are in colour. She says it must be a formatting error, and notes down that she will check. It does not get checked, but somebody asked.`,m:50,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_schweigen_umf:{txt:`You stay silent. The boss answers in your place: IT is 'very stretched'. Sabine notes down 'stretched'. For the first time somebody in this building describes the state of your department correctly, and of all people it was him.`,m:40,l:10,a:0,b:-5},res_foto:{txt:`You photograph the slide. Sabine asks what for. You say: 'For the records.' She finds that professional. In March the slide will turn up in a different presentation, in altered form, and you will have the original.`,m:45,l:-5,a:0,b:10}}},{id:`meet_video_1`,title:`Live Link to the South`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`The screen shows a meeting room that looks like yours, only brighter. Four people are sitting there, one of them stands up and waves. 'There they are!' says the boss. 'The colleagues from the south.' There is no sound. The colleagues from the south move their mouths.`,opts:[{t:`Sort the sound out`,next:`ton`},{t:`Wait and see whether it sorts itself out`,next:`abwarten`}]},root_gala:{text:`The screen shows a meeting room that looks like yours, only brighter. 'There they are, the colleagues from the south!' Downstairs the gala is already starting, up here there is no sound. The colleagues from the south move their mouths.`,opts:[{t:`Sort the sound out`,next:`ton`},{t:`Wait and see whether it sorts itself out`,next:`abwarten`}]},ton:{text:`The microphone in the south is muted, recognisable by the red symbol that nobody down there can see, because down there everybody is looking at their own camera. You type it into the chat. After twenty seconds somebody reads it out — aloud, in the silent room.`,opts:[{t:`Wait until they find it themselves`,next:`selbst`},{t:`Ring the south and tell them`,next:`anrufen`}]},abwarten:{text:`It does not sort itself out. The boss begins to speak into the screen, slowly and with exaggerated clarity, the way people speak to those who cannot hear them. In the south everybody nods pleasantly.`,opts:[{t:`Let him carry on`,next:`weitermachen`},{t:`Step in now`,next:`eingreifen`}]},selbst:{text:`They find it. After two minutes the sound comes on, in the middle of a sentence: '...I mean, put it this way, the way things run round here.' Everyone in the south freezes. The boss asks what was just said.`,opts:[{t:`'Technical fault. Not relevant.'`,next:`res_deckel`},{t:`Repeat what you heard`,next:`res_wiederholen`},{t:`Switch the sound off again`,next:`res_ton_aus`}]},anrufen:{text:`You ring the south. It rings for a long time. Then a colleague answers, audibly from the same room you can see on the screen. 'Mr Leuchter is in a meeting just now,' she says. On the screen Mr Leuchter stands up and waves at the camera.`,opts:[{t:`Explain it to her without laughing`,next:`res_erklaeren_sued`},{t:`'I can see him. He is waving right now.'`,next:`res_winkt`}]},weitermachen:{text:`The boss speaks for seven minutes into a silent screen. It is the most coherent address you have ever heard from him — no interruption, no follow-up question, no contradiction. In the south somebody has meanwhile held up a sheet of paper: NO SOUND.`,opts:[{t:`Point at the sheet`,next:`res_blatt`},{t:`Let him bring the address to an end`,next:`res_vortrag`}]},eingreifen:{text:`You cut in mid-sentence and say that the south cannot hear anything. The boss looks you over, then the screen, then you again. 'Since when?' Since the start. 'And why are you only saying so now?'`,opts:[{t:`Answer the question`,next:`res_antwort`},{t:`Fix the sound instead of answering`,next:`res_reparieren`}]}},results:{res_deckel:{txt:`'Technical fault.' The boss is satisfied with that, and so is the south. The four on the screen look at you, and one of them briefly puts a thumb up. You have never spoken to one another. Now somebody in southern Germany owes you a favour.`,m:50,l:0,a:-10,b:5},res_wiederholen:{txt:`You repeat it word for word. The south goes very quiet. The boss wants to know exactly, twice over. The second time Mr Leuchter answers politely that it had been about the quality of the coffee. It had not been about the quality of the coffee.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:5},res_erklaeren_sued:{txt:`You explain it calmly: the red symbol, the button, the order. She thanks you, hangs up, and twelve seconds later the sound comes on. As they say goodbye Mr Leuchter says your name, pronounced correctly. It is the first time.`,m:55,l:-5,a:-5,b:0},res_winkt:{txt:`'I can see him. He is waving right now.' At the other end it goes briefly quiet. Then she says: 'Oh, the link.' The sound comes on, Mr Leuchter sits down, and you and Mr Leuchter have finally spoken at the same time without ever having reached one another.`,m:45,l:0,a:-10,b:0},res_blatt:{txt:`You point at the sheet. The boss reads NO SOUND, looks at his notes, then at the seven minutes he has just spoken. 'I am not repeating that,' he says, and the meeting is over. Nobody objects.`,m:45,l:0,a:-5,b:5},res_vortrag:{txt:`He brings it to an end, and it is good. Only afterwards does the sheet get noticed. The boss asks the room whether at least somebody here had been listening. Everyone nods. No further questions are asked.`,m:55,l:5,a:5,b:-5},res_antwort:{txt:`'I thought somebody would notice.' The boss says: 'Somebody did notice. You.' He is right about that, and you will be carrying that sentence around with you for a while.`,m:45,l:0,a:10,b:5},res_reparieren:{txt:`You do not answer, you act. Two sentences into the chat, one call, sound. The boss forgets the question over the solution, as ever, and the south talks at last — forty minutes, without a break, about matters that do not concern you.`,m:60,l:-5,a:5,b:-5},res_ton_aus:{txt:`You switch the sound off again before the sentence is finished. On the screen you can see Mr Leuchter close his eyes in relief. The boss asks what is wrong. 'The line,' you say. Nobody argues with a line.`,m:45,l:5,a:-10,b:0}}},{id:`meet_leer_1`,title:`Meeting Without Attendees`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`The meeting room is empty. The projector is running and showing the start screen. On the table stand twelve glasses of water, all untouched, in a row. The meeting is in the calendar, with you as the only attendee who has accepted.`,opts:[{t:`Stay seated and wait`,next:`warten`},{t:`Look up who else was invited`,next:`einladung`}]},root_gala:{text:`The meeting room is empty. The projector is running, twelve glasses of water stand untouched in a row. From downstairs you can already hear the music of the gala. The meeting is in the calendar, with you as the only attendee who has accepted.`,opts:[{t:`Stay seated and wait`,next:`warten`},{t:`Look up who else was invited`,next:`einladung`}]},warten:{text:`After eleven minutes somebody you do not know comes in, looks around and says: 'Ah, it is not in here either.' Then he leaves again. After a further four minutes Gabi comes in with a stack of post and asks whether the room is free.`,opts:[{t:`'I have a meeting in here.'`,next:`termin_verteidigen`},{t:`Give the room up`,next:`raum_frei`}]},einladung:{text:`Fourteen people invited. Thirteen have declined, all with the same wording: 'Regrettably unavailable at that time.' Word for word identical, punctuation included. The fourteenth is you, and you accepted because you did not read the email.`,opts:[{t:`Find out who set the meeting up`,next:`urheber`},{t:`Decline as well, retrospectively`,next:`rueckwirkend`}]},termin_verteidigen:{text:`'Who with?' asks Gabi, kindly and without mockery. You look at the calendar. You look at the twelve glasses. Gabi waits with her stack of post and evidently has time.`,opts:[{t:`'With me.'`,next:`res_mit_mir`},{t:`Ask her whether she knows what sort of meeting this is`,next:`res_gabi_weiss`},{t:`Go and leave the room to her`,next:`res_ueberlassen`}]},raum_frei:{text:`You give the room up. Gabi puts the stack of post down, sorts it standing and talks while she does. After ten minutes you know who is resigning next week, why the copier on the third floor is being taken out and that the meeting has been happening weekly for months.`,opts:[{t:`Ask about the meeting`,next:`res_serientermin`},{t:`Ask about the copier`,next:`res_kopierer`}]},urheber:{text:`The meeting was set up by an account that appears only as initials. No full name, no department, no reply address. The recurring meeting has been running for fourteen months, weekly, on Friday afternoons, and nobody has ever deleted it.`,opts:[{t:`Delete the recurring meeting`,next:`res_loeschen`},{t:`Leave it standing and prepare the refusal`,next:`res_absage`}]},rueckwirkend:{text:`You decline retrospectively, with the same wording as the other thirteen. The confirmation arrives at once. Two seconds later a second email arrives: the organiser has moved the meeting to next Friday, same time, same room.`,opts:[{t:`Decline the next one as well`,next:`res_naechster`},{t:`Leave it at that and go`,next:`res_gehen`}]}},results:{res_mit_mir:{txt:`'With me.' Gabi nods seriously, puts the stack of post down and sits with you for two minutes. She says nothing, you say nothing, the twelve glasses stand untouched. It is the most peaceful meeting of the year.`,rep:{Gabi:5},m:45,l:0,a:-10,b:0},res_gabi_weiss:{txt:`Gabi knows, of course. 'That used to be the project round. The project finished a year ago.' She says it in passing, like the weather. She cannot delete the meeting, she says, since she is not the organiser. The organiser is nobody.`,rep:{Gabi:5},m:50,l:0,a:-5,b:0},res_serientermin:{txt:`The meeting has been running for fourteen months. In that time exactly one person has attended it: you, today. Gabi finds that funny, you find it less funny, and the projector is still running.`,m:45,l:10,a:-5,b:0},res_kopierer:{txt:`The copier on the third floor is being taken out because according to the statistics it is used too rarely. The statistics count only jobs sent over the network. The copier on the third floor is not on the network. That explains the statistics completely.`,m:50,l:-5,a:10,b:0},res_loeschen:{txt:`You delete the recurring meeting. It takes four seconds and ends fourteen months. On Monday nobody asks about it. On the Friday after next somebody in the corridor notices that he suddenly has time, and he does not know why.`,m:45,l:-5,a:-5,b:5},res_absage:{txt:`You leave it standing and save the refusal as a template. Next Friday you will need it again, and the Friday after that. It is the most efficient solution and the wrong one, and you know both.`,m:40,l:10,a:0,b:0},res_naechster:{txt:`You decline the next one as well. The confirmation comes, the postponement comes, the circle closes. Somewhere a calendar entry is idling and will outlive the lot of you.`,m:40,l:5,a:5,b:0},res_gehen:{txt:`You leave. The projector goes on running, the twelve glasses go on standing, and next Friday both will be the same again. Out in the corridor you wonder briefly whether you should have switched the projector off, and go back.`,m:40,l:10,a:-5,b:0},res_ueberlassen:{txt:`You leave the room to Gabi and go. Out in the corridor you hear her on the phone behind you, loud and in good spirits. The projector goes on running, the twelve glasses go on standing, and at least somebody is using the meeting now.`,m:35,l:5,a:-5,b:0}}},{id:`meet_audit_1`,title:`Preparing for the Audit`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`Ms Elster is already seated, with two folders and a note with something crossed out on it. 'The external audit is coming in March,' says the boss. 'We shall go through the list.' The list has eighteen points. Fourteen of them concern IT.`,opts:[{t:`Go through the fourteen points one by one`,next:`punkte`},{t:`Ask who drew the list up`,next:`liste`}]},root_gala:{text:`Ms Elster is already seated, with two folders and a note with something crossed out on it. 'The external audit is coming in March,' says the boss, glancing downstairs, where the gala is starting. 'We shall go through the list. Briskly.' Fourteen of the eighteen points concern IT.`,opts:[{t:`Go through the fourteen points one by one`,next:`punkte`},{t:`Ask who drew the list up`,next:`liste`}]},punkte:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`Point one: access rights documented. Point two: password policy implemented. Point three: leavers' accounts disabled. At point three Ms Elster looks up from her folder and waits to see whether you say anything.`,opts:[{t:`Be honest about point three`,next:`punkt_drei`},{t:`Tick point three off and move on`,next:`abhaken`}]},liste:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`'The list is from the audit firm,' says Ms Elster. 'From the last audit.' She lays it beside a second, identical one. 'That is the one from the audit before. The points are the same.' The boss says that in that case they are familiar.`,opts:[{t:`Ask why they are open twice over`,next:`zweimal`},{t:`Offer to work through the fourteen points by March`,next:`anbieten`}]},punkt_drei:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`You say that at least one account is still active. Ms Elster nods as though she had been waiting for it, and opens a page she has evidently prepared. It is not one account. It is seven.`,opts:[{t:`Undertake to clear them before the audit`,next:`res_bereinigen`},{t:`Say that somebody has to decide who is responsible`,next:`res_zustaendig`},{t:`Ask Ms Elster what she would do in your place`,next:`res_elster_rat`}]},abhaken:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`You tick it off. Ms Elster puts no tick. She writes a small question mark in the margin, in pencil, and turns the page. At point seven she does the same. At point eleven as well.`,opts:[{t:`Raise the question marks`,next:`res_fragezeichen`},{t:`Carry on ticking`,next:`res_weiter`}]},zweimal:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`'Because they have been promised twice and never done.' She says it without reproach, in the tone of a weather forecast. The boss clears his throat. The promises are from two predecessors, and neither is in the building any more.`,opts:[{t:`This time promise only what can be managed`,next:`res_realistisch`},{t:`Promise all fourteen, like the predecessors`,next:`res_alle`}]},anbieten:{char:`Frau Elster`,text:`The boss breathes out audibly and says 'very good' twice. Ms Elster notes it down. Then she asks, without looking up: 'And alongside the day job?' It is the only honest question asked in this room today.`,opts:[{t:`'Alongside the day job.'`,next:`res_neben`},{t:`Say what would be left undone for it`,next:`res_liegenbleiben`}]}},results:{res_bereinigen:{txt:`You undertake to disable the seven accounts. Ms Elster notes down the date and time of your undertaking — not out of mistrust, but because she notes everything down. In March she will open that page, and you will want to see a tick there.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:55,l:-5,a:5,b:0},res_zustaendig:{txt:`'For that somebody has to decide who is responsible.' A short silence. The boss says that is surely obvious. Ms Elster asks: 'Who, then?' The boss does not answer, and the question stands in the room until the end of the meeting.`,m:50,l:0,a:5,b:10},res_fragezeichen:{txt:`'What do the question marks mean?' Ms Elster looks up. 'That I shall be checking it.' She says it pleasantly. It is the most polite threat anybody has ever made to you, and she does not mean it as one.`,m:45,l:0,a:10,b:0},res_weiter:{txt:`You tick off fourteen points in eleven minutes. The boss is satisfied, the minutes are short, and there are now nine question marks in the margin of Ms Elster's list. She will check every one of them. She has until March.`,m:40,l:10,a:5,b:-5},res_realistisch:{txt:`You undertake four of fourteen, with dates. The boss thinks four is few. Ms Elster thinks four is realistic and writes it into the minutes that way. In March there are four completed points — the first in two audits.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":10},m:50,l:-5,a:0,b:5},res_alle:{txt:`You undertake all fourteen. The boss is delighted and says so. Ms Elster writes it down without saying anything, and even as she writes you know that in March she will have a third identical list.`,m:45,l:5,a:10,b:-10},res_neben:{txt:`'Alongside the day job.' Ms Elster nods and writes it into the minutes exactly like that: alongside the day job. The sentence will be read in March, by people who know what it means.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:-5},res_liegenbleiben:{txt:`You list what will be left undone for it: the ticket backlog, server maintenance, two migrations. The boss listens to the halfway point. Ms Elster listens to the end and takes all of it down. One of those two is worth more.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:55,l:-5,a:5,b:5},res_elster_rat:{txt:`'What would you do in my place?' Ms Elster looks up, surprised, and actually gives it thought. 'Four points. In writing. With dates.' Then, more quietly: 'And the other ten next year, with the same sentence.'`,rep:{"Frau Elster":10},m:50,l:-5,a:-5,b:0}}},{id:`meet_ki_1`,title:`Pilot Project: Assistance`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`On the screen a chat window is typing by itself. 'This is Aiden,' says the consultant. 'He answers tickets.' The boss beams. The slide underneath says what Aiden was trained on: the ticket replies of the last three years. Yours.`,opts:[{t:`Ask who signed the replies off`,next:`freigabe`},{t:`Have Aiden put to a question`,next:`test`}]},root_gala:{text:`On the screen a chat window is typing by itself. 'This is Aiden. He answers tickets.' Downstairs the gala is starting, up here the boss is beaming. The slide says what Aiden was trained on: the ticket replies of the last three years. Yours.`,opts:[{t:`Ask who signed the replies off`,next:`freigabe`},{t:`Have Aiden put to a question`,next:`test`}]},freigabe:{text:`'Signed off?' The consultant looks over at the boss, who looks at his papers. 'They are company data, after all,' says the consultant eventually. The sentence is true and does not answer the question. In the chat window Aiden goes on typing.`,opts:[{t:`Press the point`,next:`nachhaken`},{t:`Let it stand and watch what Aiden writes`,next:`zusehen`}]},test:{text:`The consultant types a specimen question: 'My printer is not working.' Aiden answers in two seconds: 'Is the device switched on? Please check the cable, and the paper tray first.' It is your standard sentence word for word, including the comma that sits wrongly in it.`,opts:[{t:`Point out the comma`,next:`komma`},{t:`Suggest a harder question`,next:`schwierig`}]},nachhaken:{text:`You ask a second time, calmly and in one sentence. The consultant says the legal department will settle that. You have no legal department. The boss says Accounts will settle it, and means Ms Elster, who knows nothing about it.`,opts:[{t:`Leave it be and have the question minuted`,next:`res_protokoll_ki`},{t:`Say that there is no legal department`,next:`res_keine_rechts`}]},zusehen:{text:`Aiden answers four tickets in eleven seconds. Three answers are good. The fourth recommends that a customer restart the server, and it is the production server, and the recommendation sounds exactly like you on a bad Friday.`,opts:[{t:`Read the fourth answer out to the room`,next:`res_vierte`},{t:`Quietly take it out of the training material later`,next:`res_still`},{t:`Ask the consultant who is liable for the answers`,next:`res_haftung`}]},komma:{text:`'The comma is in the wrong place.' The consultant smiles politely. 'He will learn that yet.' Then he grasps what you said, and looks at the answer again. The boss cannot follow this. Is that good now, or bad?`,opts:[{t:`'It is my comma.'`,next:`res_mein_komma`},{t:`'It is good. That is the problem.'`,next:`res_gut_problem`}]},schwierig:{text:`You type in a real question from this week: the business with the label printing and the umlauts. Aiden answers at length, pleasantly, in four paragraphs. None of it is right. The boss finds the answer very well structured.`,opts:[{t:`Put the right answer beside it`,next:`res_daneben`},{t:`Let the boss praise the answer`,next:`res_loben`}]}},results:{res_protokoll_ki:{txt:`The question is in the minutes, unanswered, with your name in front of it. That is little and more than nothing. Aiden goes into pilot operation in January, and your question goes with him.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:-5},res_keine_rechts:{txt:`'We have no legal department.' The consultant leafs through, finds nothing and notes it down. The boss says somebody will be 'brought in'. Three weeks later a solicitor really is in the building, and she asks exactly your question.`,rep:{"Frau Elster":5},m:55,l:0,a:10,b:5},res_vierte:{txt:`You read the fourth answer out, as far as the part with the restart. The consultant says that is a training artefact. The boss asks what a production server is. Aiden goes into pilot operation regardless, but with an approval step in front of it.`,m:55,l:-5,a:5,b:5},res_still:{txt:`You say nothing and quietly take the answer out of the material that evening. Nobody finds out, nobody thanks you for it, and in January a pilot starts with one catastrophe fewer in it. It is the kind of work there is no line in the minutes for.`,m:45,l:-10,a:0,b:0},res_mein_komma:{txt:`'It is my comma.' The room is briefly silent. The consultant says that only goes to show how well the model captures the house voice. He means it as a compliment, and in a certain sense it is one. You leave the meeting differently from the way you came in, all the same.`,m:50,l:0,a:15,b:0},res_gut_problem:{txt:`'It is good. That is the problem.' The boss gets it on the second attempt and goes quiet. The consultant clicks to the next slide, which has a savings potential on it. He skips it without explaining it, and everyone has seen it.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:5},res_daneben:{txt:`You put the right answer beside it: two sentences, one tick box in the print settings. The difference is plain to see on the screen. The consultant photographs the slide. He says that is valuable training material, and means you.`,m:55,l:-5,a:0,b:5},res_loben:{txt:`The boss praises the answer at length, the structure especially. Aiden has written four paragraphs about something he does not know, and was praised for it. You recognise in that a career model that works well in this building.`,m:45,l:5,a:5,b:-5},res_haftung:{txt:`'Who is liable for the answers?' The consultant says the model is a tool. You ask who is liable for a tool. He says: the user. Everyone in the room looks at you, and you are the user.`,m:50,l:0,a:10,b:5}}},{id:`meet_kultur_1`,title:`Meeting Culture Workshop`,startNode:`root`,startNodeGala:`root_gala`,nodes:{root:{text:`'We have too many meetings,' says the boss. 'Hence this meeting.' Beside him sits a consultant with an analysis: one hundred and forty hours of meeting time a month, across the building. Slide two says how many of those were rated 'necessary'. There are twenty-one.`,opts:[{t:`Ask which category this meeting falls into`,next:`kategorie`},{t:`Ask about the other one hundred and nineteen hours`,next:`restliche`}]},root_gala:{text:`'We have too many meetings. Hence this meeting.' The boss says it without a trace of irony, while downstairs the gala is starting. Beside him a consultant with an analysis: one hundred and forty hours of meeting time a month. Necessary among them: twenty-one.`,opts:[{t:`Ask which category this meeting falls into`,next:`kategorie`},{t:`Ask about the other one hundred and nineteen hours`,next:`restliche`}]},kategorie:{text:`The consultant is the only one who laughs. 'Good point.' Then she turns serious: 'This meeting is an investment.' She writes INVESTMENT on the flipchart and underneath it a figure with a euro sign that means the daily rate and that nobody comments on.`,opts:[{t:`Comment on the figure`,next:`zahl_kommentieren`},{t:`Ask about the rules she is proposing`,next:`regeln`}]},restliche:{text:`'The remaining hours are spread across alignments, standing meetings and recurring meetings.' She clicks on. A bar chart, sorted by department. The longest bar belongs to Sales. The second longest to a department called 'Other'.`,opts:[{t:`Ask who 'Other' is`,next:`sonstige`},{t:`Press the point about recurring meetings`,next:`serientermine`}]},zahl_kommentieren:{text:`You do the sum out loud: the daily rate against the twenty-one necessary hours. The consultant listens calmly and then says: 'Which is why we are only doing this once.' The boss nods. The follow-up meeting is already in the calendar, in four weeks' time.`,opts:[{t:`Point out the follow-up meeting`,next:`res_folgetermin`},{t:`Leave it be`,next:`res_belassen`}]},regeln:{text:`Three rules. One: no meetings without an agenda. Two: no meetings over thirty minutes. Three: anybody who cannot contribute declines. The rules are good. This meeting breaks all three.`,opts:[{t:`Say so`,next:`res_verstoss`},{t:`Take the rules down and apply them yourself`,next:`res_anwenden`},{t:`Ask whether the rules apply to Management as well`,next:`res_auch_gf`}]},sonstige:{text:`'Other' is the catch-all category for everything that could not be assigned to a department. The consultant opens the detail view. The largest item in it bears the name of your department, misspelled, with a hyphen.`,opts:[{t:`Put the spelling right`,next:`res_schreibweise`},{t:`Ask whether that changes the analysis`,next:`res_auswertung`}]},serientermine:{text:`'Recurring meetings are the real subject,' you say. The consultant agrees at once and shows a figure: three hundred and eleven active recurring meetings in the building. For eighty of them the organiser is no longer with the company.`,opts:[{t:`Offer to clear the eighty out`,next:`res_bereinigen_serien`},{t:`Ask why nobody has done it yet`,next:`res_warum`}]}},results:{res_folgetermin:{txt:`'The follow-up meeting is in the calendar.' The consultant checks and nods appreciatively. 'For the effectiveness review.' She says it without hesitating, and in that moment you understand that she is very good indeed at her job.`,m:50,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_belassen:{txt:`You let it lie. The figure stays on the flipchart for the whole meeting, and nobody looks at it again. While packing up, the consultant tears the sheet off and takes it with her.`,m:45,l:10,a:0,b:-5},res_verstoss:{txt:`'This meeting breaks all three.' It is silent. Then the consultant laughs, honestly and loudly, and says: 'Yes.' The meeting ends forty minutes earlier than planned. It is the only Friday on which you leave the building before Egon.`,m:35,l:0,a:-10,b:5},res_anwenden:{txt:`You take the three rules down and apply them from Monday. Your own meetings get shorter, and twice you decline because you have nothing to contribute. The second time the boss asks why you were absent.`,m:50,l:-5,a:5,b:5},res_schreibweise:{txt:`You put the spelling right. The consultant corrects it in the file, and the item moves out of 'Other' into your department. Your department's bar is then the second longest in the building. The boss looks at it very closely indeed.`,m:50,l:0,a:5,b:10},res_auswertung:{txt:`'Does that change the analysis?' The consultant checks. It changes it considerably: two departments swap places, and Sales is suddenly no longer top. She says she will recalculate it. The new version is never seen by anybody.`,m:50,l:-5,a:0,b:10},res_bereinigen_serien:{txt:`You offer to clear out the eighty orphaned recurring meetings. The boss thinks that is splendid. The consultant notes it down as an outcome of the workshop, and the report says later that the workshop saved eighty meetings. Exactly one person worked on it.`,m:55,l:-10,a:5,b:0},res_warum:{txt:`'Why has nobody done that yet?' The consultant looks at the boss. The boss buries himself in his papers. After a while she says: 'Because nobody is responsible.' She writes RESPONSIBILITY on the flipchart, underlined twice.`,m:45,l:0,a:5,b:5},res_auch_gf:{txt:`'Do the rules apply to Management as well?' The consultant looks at the boss. The boss says 'naturally', in a tone that means something else. She writes it down anyway, word for word, with his name in front of it.`,m:45,l:0,a:5,b:10}}}];export{e as meetings};